    'Management of a raised INR depends on the level and on whether there is bleeding. With no bleeding, warfarin is withheld, the cause sought (drug interactions, illness, diet, alcohol) and the INR rechecked; for an INR above 5–8 a small oral dose of vitamin K (1–2.5 mg) may be given, especially with high bleeding risk, and above 8 it usually is. Major bleeding requires prothrombin complex concentrate (or fresh frozen plasma if unavailable) plus intravenous vitamin K 5–10 mg. Large doses of vitamin K are avoided in patients with mechanical valves when possible, because prolonged over-correction risks valve thrombosis; anticoagulation is restarted promptly once safe.',
