    'Löfgren syndrome — acute sarcoidosis with fever, bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, erythema nodosum and arthralgia or arthritis (often of the ankles) — carries a good prognosis, with spontaneous remission in most patients within 1–2 years, needing only NSAIDs or a short steroid course for symptoms. Sarcoidosis is a multisystem granulomatous disease; hypercalcaemia arises from macrophage 1α-hydroxylase activity, and ACE is raised but non-specific. Indications for systemic corticosteroids include progressive pulmonary disease, cardiac or neurological involvement, hypercalcaemia and sight-threatening eye disease. Lupus pernio and chronic fibrotic lung disease indicate chronic, less remitting disease.',
