    'In CKD, phosphate retention, low calcitriol and hypocalcaemia drive secondary hyperparathyroidism and CKD–mineral and bone disorder, which causes bone pain, fractures, vascular calcification and pruritus. Management combines dietary phosphate restriction (processed food and cola are major sources), adequate dialysis, and phosphate binders — sevelamer or lanthanum when calcium is high or there is vascular calcification, calcium-based binders only if calcium is low-normal. Active vitamin D analogues and calcimimetics (cinacalcet) lower PTH, with parathyroidectomy for refractory disease. Aluminium binders are avoided because of bone and neurological toxicity.',
