    'Biliary colic — episodic severe right upper quadrant or epigastric pain lasting hours, often after meals, with normal inflammatory markers and liver tests — indicates symptomatic gallstones, for which elective laparoscopic cholecystectomy is the treatment of choice because symptoms recur and complications (cholecystitis, pancreatitis, choledocholithiasis) develop in a substantial proportion. Asymptomatic gallstones are generally left alone, except in selected cases (porcelain gallbladder, large polyps, stones over 3 cm, some patients awaiting transplantation). Oral dissolution therapy is rarely effective. ERCP is reserved for bile duct stones, suggested by jaundice, cholangitis or a dilated duct.',
